direction: right

title: |md
  ## bpf_dynptr: dynamic-size writes to ringbuf
| {near: top-center; shape: text}

problem: "Problem with reserve():" {
  style.fill: "#fee2e2"
  p_body: |md
    bpf_ringbuf_reserve(rb, sz, 0)
    requires `sz` to be **constant** at
    BPF compile time.

    What if you don't know the size yet?
    (e.g., variable-length string,
     packet payload of unknown length)

    Old solutions:
    - Reserve max size, waste tail bytes
    - Use bpf_ringbuf_output (always copies)
    - Keep size capped at small max
  |
}

solution: "Solution: bpf_ringbuf_reserve_dynptr" {
  style.fill: "#dcfce7"
  s_body: |md
    ```c
    struct bpf_dynptr ptr;
    bpf_ringbuf_reserve_dynptr(&rb, sz, 0, &ptr);

    /* dynamically write to the dynptr: */
    bpf_dynptr_write(&ptr, 0, &header, sizeof(header));
    bpf_dynptr_write(&ptr, sizeof(header), payload, payload_len);

    bpf_ringbuf_submit_dynptr(&ptr, 0);
    ```

    ✅ Variable size at runtime
    ✅ Verifier tracks bounds via dynptr type
    ✅ Same throughput as direct reserve
  |
}

verifier: "Verifier tracking" {
  style.fill: "#fef3c7"
  v_body: |md
    The Verifier tracks `bpf_dynptr` as
    a special pointer type with bounds.

    - `bpf_dynptr_write(p, off, src, len)`
      checks `off + len ≤ p.size`
    - same for `bpf_dynptr_read`

    Caller can't OOB-write even if
    `off`/`len` come from packet data.
  |
}

problem -> solution
solution -> verifier
